{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GD4wQXbZgZMwQaWnQAAVeT","type":"tool_result","content":"160:\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n164:generator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\n169:rules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\n185:from the generator's parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor} gives, for each of\n215:padding and the rejection break. Measured by simulation under the fitted\n216:generator, both make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a\n220:proof; a proof would couple the padded instance to the unpadded one, which\n====\n9:## 1. The map: every instance on record placed by its excess (item 01, 2026-10-31)\n49:### 1.1 Findings\n144:### 1.2 Kill verdict\n153:### 1.3 What this does not claim\n164:### 1.4 Figure\n174:### 1.5 Tables, in full\n887:## 2. `opt / n` against the excess: one parameter, or two? (item 02, 2026-11-01)\n935:### 2.1 Findings\n1024:### 2.2 Kill verdict\n1035:### 2.3 What this does not claim\n1055:### 2.4 Figure\n1068:### 2.5 Tables, in full\n1282:## 3. The forest regime, by brute force (item 03, 2026-11-01)\n1378:### 3.1 Findings\n1484:### 3.2 Kill verdict\n1504:### 3.3 What this does not claim\n1526:### 3.4 Tables, in full\n1881:### 3.5 The two censored random block trees, rerun for an hour each\n1903:## 4. Industrial instances: the 24 SCOOP instances in the excess (item 04, 2026-11-01)\n1984:### 4.1 Findings\n2090:### 4.2 The planner's statement (a draft of the paper's paragraph)\n2134:### 4.3 Kill verdict\n2144:### 4.4 What this does not claim\n2165:### 4.5 Tables, in full\n2302:## 5. `β⁺` by simulation: the width of explicit constructions at large `n` (item 05, 2026-11-01)\n2382:### 5.1 Findings\n2504:### 5.2 Kill verdict\n2521:### 5.3 What this does not claim\n2544:### 5.4 Figure\n2554:### 5.5 Tables, in full\n2877:## 6. The forest regime, on paper (item 06, 2026-11-01)\n2929:### 6.1 Statements\n2984:### 6.2 Proofs\n3181:### 6.3 The brute-force check\n3287:### 6.4 Findings\n3330:### 6.5 Lean-ready statements for item 11\n3363:### 6.6 Kill verdict\n3370:### 6.7 What this does not claim\n3393:### 6.8 Tables, in full\n3419:## 7. `β⁺` on paper: the provable constructions analysed (item 07, 2026-11-01)\n3464:### 7.1 The layer lemma (deterministic)\n3497:### 7.2 The exploration recursion for the layer fractions\n3578:### 7.3 Greedy independent sets, and the slot picture\n3641:### 7.4 The recursions against simulation\n3713:### 7.5 `β⁺(d, μ)` on the uniform model, beside `β*`, `b` and the rule\n3752:### 7.6 Findings\n3827:### 7.7 Tables, in full\n3938:### 7.8 Kill verdict\n3952:### 7.9 What this does not claim\n3981:## 8. The structured count: can a richer first moment lift `β*`? (item 08, 2026-11-01)\n4012:### 8.1 The structure, and that a layout forces it (checked)\n4068:### 8.2 `β*_ℓ` at equal spacing, `ℓ ≤ 4`\n4104:### 8.3 Every spacing, not just equal spacing (`ℓ = 2`), and the limit\n4164:### 8.4 The finite floors on the 200 Chu & Stuckey instances (`ℓ = 2`)\n4242:### 8.5 The diagnostic: where the slack is\n4357:### 8.6 Findings\n4390:### 8.7 Kill verdict\n4399:### 8.8 What this does not claim\n4427:## 9. The ridge mechanism: one hypothesis, one test (item 09, 2026-11-01)\n4466:### 9.1 Method\n4495:### 9.2 The test\n4536:### 9.3 What the gap does along the density grid\n4579:### 9.4 The secondary: optimal closing orders at `n = 20`\n4669:### 9.5 Findings\n4690:### 9.6 Kill verdict\n4699:### 9.7 What this does not claim\n====\n| fixed       |  20 |   10.00 |      10.00 |  9.00 |    0.97 |         150 |              0.00 |                        0.00 |                               17.78 |                    -0.60 |                               17.78 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    2.00 |       2.00 |  2.00 |    0.31 |         150 |              1.92 |                        2.01 |                               11.68 |                    -6.71 |                               10.50 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    3.00 |       3.00 |  4.00 |    0.58 |         150 |              1.60 |                        1.61 |                               13.48 |                    -4.91 |                               12.99 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    4.00 |       4.00 |  6.00 |    0.74 |         150 |              1.13 |                        1.13 |                               14.81 |                    -3.57 |                               14.73 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    5.00 |       5.00 |  8.00 |    0.84 |         150 |              0.70 |                        0.70 |                               16.10 |                    -2.28 |                               16.07 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    6.00 |       6.00 | 10.00 |    0.91 |         150 |              0.30 |                        0.30 |                               16.74 |                    -1.65 |                               16.74 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    7.00 |       7.00 | 12.00 |    0.96 |         150 |              0.00 |                        0.00 |                               17.56 |                    -0.82 |                               17.56 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    8.00 |       8.00 | 14.00 |    0.99 |         150 |              0.00 |                        0.00 |                               18.39 |                     0.00 |                               18.39 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |    9.00 |       9.00 | 16.00 |    1.00 |         150 |              0.00 |                        0.00 |                               18.39 |                     0.00 |                               18.39 |                 150 |\n| fixed       |  40 |   10.00 |      10.00 | 18.00 |    1.00 |         150 |              0.00 |                        0.00 |                               18.39 |                     0.00 |                               18.39 |                 150 |\n\n\n### 9.5 Findings\n\n1. **H1 is dead** on both readings of the floor: 22 and 23 of 25 series\n   outside the node peak's interval, mean scores 0.109 and 0.135 decades,\n   the miss one-sided toward the dense side (excess 2.2–5.3 at the gap's\n   maximum against 1.8–3.0 at the ridge).\n2. **The ridge sits on the gap's plateau, not at its top.** On every\n   series the floor gap at the ridge is within one stack (0.000–0.022 per\n   customer) of its maximum; the plateau spans `e ≈ 2–5`, twice the width\n   of the ridge's own interval, and the ridge is on its sparse flank. This\n   is consistent with the record (§2: the bound gap peaks on the ridge\n   *and* on its dense shoulder) and is not a mechanism: it does not pick\n   the ridge out of the plateau.\n3. **The gap's location is less constant across ratios than the excess**\n   (CV 0.24 against 0.15) and its height orders the ratios backwards from\n   the ridge's height (finding 3 of §9.3).\n4. **Free audit.** Zero violations of the 0.01 floor on 9,497 certified\n   random covers at 50–75; 19 of the bound-below-1 floor, one stack each\n   but one.\n5. **The lattice at `n = 20`**: §9.4.\n\n### 9.6 Kill verdict\n\n**Met — H1 is dead.** Outside the interval on 22 of 25 (floor) and 23 of\n25 (`β*`) series against the 12 the kill allows; mean scores 0.109 and\n0.135 decades (the kill's 0.15 is passed by the floor reading alone, and the\ninterval test is failed by both). A dead H1 is the result the item asked\nfor. No certified value is wrong, no theorem is false, no published claim\nof ours drifts.\n\n### 9.7 What this does not claim\n\n- **A mechanism.** A coincidence of peaks on 25 series would have been\n  evidence for one; the absence of a coincidence is evidence against\n  *this* one and says nothing about what the mechanism is. The derivation\n  of the reachable count in the random model (§36 (f)) is the open problem\n  and is not attempted.\n- **That the gap is unrelated to hardness.** The gap and the nodes rise\n  together from the sparse edge on every series, and the ridge is within a\n  stack of the gap's maximum; what is dead is the claim that the gap's\n  *maximum* locates the ridge.\n- **Anything about the floor's slack as a bound.** The gap here is a\n  property of where `β*` is loose (few, large patterns; §8's clustering),\n  not of the search.\n- **Anything above 75 customers**, or about the padded generators' limit:\n  the finite floors are the uniform-model statement at the instance's own\n  sizes, as Appendix E states them.\n- **That the secondary count is peaked** — see §9.4 for what it is.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec075f6c-5313-4bc9-8854-91bff469a12d","uuid":"4f9f430e-b1c2-4844-b2d0-254d56f03c1d","timestamp":"2026-11-01T06:19:51.938Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"160:\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n164:generator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\n169:rules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\n185:from the generator's parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor} gives, for each of\n215:padding and the rejection break. Measured by simulation under the fitted\n216:generator, both make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a\n220:proof; a proof would couple the padded instance to the unpadded one, which\n====\n9:## 1. The map: every instance on record placed by its excess (item 01, 2026-10-31)\n49:### 1.1 Findings\n144:### 1.2 Kill verdict\n153:### 1.3 What this does not claim\n164:### 1.4 Figure\n174:### 1.5 Tables, in full\n887:## 2. `opt / n` against the excess: one parameter, or two? (item 02, 2026-11-01)\n935:### 2.1 Findings\n1024:### 2.2 Kill verdict\n1035:### 2.3 What this does not claim\n1055:### 2.4 Figure\n1068:### 2.5 Tables, in full\n1282:## 3. The forest regime, by brute force (item 03, 2026-11-01)\n1378:### 3.1 Findings\n1484:### 3.2 Kill verdict\n1504:### 3.3 What this doe