{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KiNn41Qmijh5gkmAqKswCK","type":"tool_result","content":"Substituting $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ into $g(a)=0$ gives the equation of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, and the asymptotic form follows from the\ntwo-sided estimate $2\\ln(1/a)-ca\\le g(a)/a\\le2\\ln(1/a)+2-ca$. This part is\nproved on paper only.\n\n\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n\\label{app:random-cs}\n\nThe random instances of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are not $G(n,p)$. The\ngenerator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\nindependent entries at a density chosen to give $d$ customers per pattern on\naverage, and discards any instance whose MOSP graph is disconnected; the\nfiles show two further rules the paper does not state, every customer given\nat least one pattern and every pattern at least two customers, and with those\nrules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\nclasses. The MOSP graph of such an instance is the union of one clique per\npattern. Steps 1 and 2 of the proof survive this change unaltered: a graph of\npathwidth $k$ still has an empty pair, and a pair is empty exactly when no\npattern contains a customer of each side. If the patterns are independent\nrandom customer sets of sizes $s_1,\\dots,s_m$, the probability that a given\npair $(A,R)$ with $|A|=t$, $|R|=t-1$ is empty factorises over the patterns,\nand the union bound gives\n\\[\n  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\n  \\frac{\\binom{n-t}{s_j}+\\binom{n-t+1}{s_j}-\\binom{n-2t+1}{s_j}}{\\binom{n}{s_j}},\n\\]\nthe fraction being the share of $s_j$-sets that miss $A$ or miss $R$. This is\na Lean theorem in the paper's own vocabulary, with the instance built from the\npatterns and the event stated as $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le k+1$~\\leanref{clique-cover}.\nIt is computable from the pattern sizes alone, so it predicts the benchmark\nfrom the generator's parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor} gives, for each of\nthe forty classes, the mean certified optimum of its five instances beside\nthe floor the inequality gives at confidence $0.01$, that is, the largest\nvalue it does not rule out at that level plus one. None of the 200 certified\noptima lies below its floor. At $d=10$ the floor is within $5\\%$ of the\noptimum, at $d=4$ within $20\\%$; at $d=2$ it is a third short, and\nasymptotically it says nothing there: with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of $d$\ncustomers, the exponent of the expected number of balanced empty pairs is\n$\\ln2\\,\\bigl(1+\\mu(1-d)\\bigr)$, so the method gives a positive constant\nexactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$. That quantity is the mean number of patterns per\ncustomer minus one, the number of ones in the table minus the number of\npatterns, per customer; call it the excess. The classes of\nSection~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the\nsparsest ones, have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the region\nwhere this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers the\ncost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2.3$ to $2.5$. Whether the\nthreshold and that peak are related is not known.\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{The Chu \\& Stuckey classes: mean certified optimum of the five\ninstances, and the floor from the first-moment inequality at confidence\n$0.01$, computed from the pattern sizes of each file. No certified optimum\nlies below its floor.}\n\\label{tab:cs-floor}\n\\small\n\\input{tables/cs_firstmoment}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\npadding and the rejection break. Measured by simulation under the fitted\ngenerator, both make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a\nfactor that falls from $1.0$ to $0.6$ as the sets grow, so the bound stays\nvalid; and on 7{,}200 fresh instances at 30 and 40 customers solved exactly,\nnone has an optimum below its $0.01$ floor. That is a measurement, not a\nproof; a proof would couple the padded instance to the unpadded one, which\nhas only more edges.\n\nThe same first moment appears in \\citet{Gao2012} for random intersection\ngraphs, with treewidth in place of pathwidth. His proof drops the factor\ncorresponding to the product above between two lines, which is harmless when\nthe number of patterns is sublinear in $n$ and changes the constant when it is\nlinear; his threshold of two patterns per customer is the dropped factor's\nartefact, and with it restored the condition at $m=n$ is about $2.46$.\n\n\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\n\nSteps 1, 2, 3 and 4 and the two inequalities of Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}\nare Lean theorems with the statement exactly as printed, including the\nconstant~3; so is the consequence that the probability tends to zero whenever\n$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. The entropy bound on trinomial coefficients is not in","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec075f6c-5313-4bc9-8854-91bff469a12d","uuid":"96691b47-ff26-4be0-b825-081d8a65a8ac","timestamp":"2026-11-01T06:24:22.325Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Substituting $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ into $g(a)=0$ gives the equation of\nTheorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, and the asymptotic form follows from the\ntwo-sided estimate $2\\ln(1/a)-ca\\le g(a)/a\\le2\\ln(1/a)+2-ca$. This part is\nproved on paper only.\n\n\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n\\label{app:random-cs}\n\nThe random instances of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are not $G(n,p)$. The\ngenerator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\nindependent entries at a density chosen to give $d$ customers per pattern on\naverage, and discards any instance whose MOSP graph is disconnected; the\nfiles show two further rules the paper does not state, every customer given\nat least one pattern and every pattern at least two customers, and with those\nrules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\nclasses