{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgbkAxhqj2ewGNzSi4hz9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C79yb99tEoXuk4Pjzo9n1B","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nf = Path(\"paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex\"); s = f.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"The inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\npadding and the rejection break. Measured by simulation under the fitted\ngenerator, both make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a\nfactor that falls from $1.0$ to $0.6$ as the sets grow, so the bound stays\nvalid; and on 7{,}200 fresh instances at 30 and 40 customers solved exactly,\nnone has an optimum below its $0.01$ floor. That is a measurement, not a\nproof; a proof would couple the padded instance to the unpadded one, which\nhas only more edges.\n\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"The inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\npadding and the rejection break. What is proved is the inequality with the\nsizes the table has \\\\emph{before} the repairs. On the Bernoulli table a given\npair is empty with probability exactly\n$[(1-p)^t+(1-p)^{t-1}-(1-p)^{2t-1}]^m$, the binomial average of the fraction\nabove; the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an\nempty pair of the original and the bound with Bernoulli sizes holds for the\npadded instance; and if the patterns are padded before the customers, the\nrows after that pad are independent uniform sets of size $\\\\max(s,2)$ with $s$\nbinomial, and the bound holds with those sizes. The rejection is not an\nincreasing event of the table entries: adding an entry can make a pad vanish,\ndisconnect the instance and lower its pathwidth, so Harris's inequality does\nnot apply to it, and conditioning on acceptance costs at most the factor\n$1/\\\\Pr[\\\\text{accepted}]$, which is measured: within $1\\\\%$ of $1$ at\n$d\\\\ge4$, $0.53$ to $0.87$ at $d=2$, and $0.005$ at the $100\\\\times50$, $d=2$\nclass. These proved floors are the ones of Table~\\\\ref{tab:cs-floor} only at\n$d=10$; at $d=2$ they are trivial, because $40\\\\%$ of the Bernoulli patterns\nhave at most one customer and the pads that make them count are exactly what\nthe proof cannot use. With the sizes the files actually have the statement is\nmeasured, not proved: under the fitted generator the repairs and the\nrejection make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a factor\nthat falls from $1.0$ to $0.6$ as the sets grow, and on 7{,}200 fresh\ninstances at 30 and 40 customers solved exactly none has an optimum below its\n$0.01$ floor.\n\"\"\"\nassert s.count(old) == 1\nf.write_text(s.replace(old, new))\nprint(\"paper edited\")\nEOF\ngrep -n \"0.53\\|within \\$1\" paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex | head","description":"Replace the paper's coupling sentence with what is proved and what is not"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4186,"cache_read_input_tokens":246652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4186,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec075f6c-5313-4bc9-8854-91bff469a12d","uuid":"1b0e924e-beeb-475f-941a-1ad85bb02c23","timestamp":"2026-11-01T06:36:51.109Z","request_id":"req_011CgbkAxL236SmzYJVdk7aU","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01C79yb99tEoXuk4Pjzo9n1B":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && python - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nf = Path(\"paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex\"); s = f.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"The inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\npadding and the rejection break. Measured by simulation under the fitted\ngenerator, both mak